{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/243140-sample-factions","id":243140,"title":"Sample factions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-08-28T08:23:43-07:00","id":243140,"title":"Sample factions","content":"Using <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/76194-mummy-reworked-society-creed-judges-decrees\">my ideas on Mummy culture and society and guilds </a>as basis, purely for the fun of it I came up with a few sample Mummy societies.<br /> <br /> KEEPERS OF THE FORGOTTEN<br /> There is no mummy who hasn't seen kingdoms, cultures and entire nations pass away, with noone other than the Arisen remembering (perhaps) what was lost.<br /> Oblivion - forgetting - is true death, as far as the Arisen are concerned, yes? Their bodies can never truly die, but if there is noone left to remember the things they've seen, did they really happen?<br /> The Keepers of the Forgotten don't just spend significant effort cultivating their own memories (skirting the edges of heresy, as far as the Arisen are concerned) - they also try to bring back memories, relics and wisdom of cultures that the world chose to forget. Not just the Nameless Empire - any sane Arisen knows that the first human empire to exist is gone forever - but all other cultures the Arisen have been part of. The Indus Valley civilization of 2500 BC. The Aksumite Empire of Ethiopia. The Minoans. The Cahokia of the Mississippi river.<br /> History is full of peoples that it chose to forget. Remembering their cultures and bringing them back to public awareness is, to the Keepers of the Forgotten, in a way, bringing them back to life.<br /> <br /> CULT OF THE NAMELESS FALCON<br /> <br /> The Arisen have seen it all, yes? Millenia passing by. Cultures dying. People starting wars for terrible reasons. All of it, happening again and again. Endless cycles.<br /> Arisen members of the heretical Cult of the Nameless Falcon (named after what also known as Horus, a god of war and hunting) refuse to let time weary their souls. Stagnation is death. Life is taking challenges. Boredom is the great enemy. The Arisen are Deathless and eternal - what reason is there for them to fear danger?<br /> Members of the Cult make it a game to face terrible danger and impossible odds. Facing the most powerful of Amkhat in single combat. Attempting to steal the most well guarded of artifacts. Giving Fate the middle finger, and feeling good about it.<br /> Noone in their right might would call them anything less than reckless and insane.<br /> That's what keeping them alive.<br /> Many of the members of the club are followers of the Heart or Spirit decree.<br /> <br /> ICONOCLASTS<br /> <br /> The greatest of all civilizations, the only true nation ever to exist, ever to be sanctioned by the Judges and gods, vanished 6000 years ago. All others to follow only attempted to copy the Nameless Empire's success, and often with disastrous results. Why should they work? None of them enjoy the blessings of the gods. All of their cities are pretenders to the glory that was Irem. In time, Fate will bring all of them down.<br /> The Iconoclasts see themselves as servants of Fate. All empires are destroyed from within, yes? They work towards insinuating their cultists into offices of power among the bureaucracies of men, subtly steering events so that none of the modern empires ever get to challenge the might and scope of the Nameless Empire. They exist to destroy society.<br /> The Judges have been known neither to punish the Iconoclasts nor to prolong their Descents. Maybe it's because the Judges are pleased with their work - subtly pushing and nudging them even as they influence their cults. Maybe the Iconoclasts do what they do because they can't bear the pain of losing the culture that birthed them, the culture that they will never see again, and all that remains is to see others suffer the same fate.<br /> <br /> BODHISATTVAS<br /> All Arisen believe that the Rite of Return, the greatest magical ritual ever to be performed, is gone. The circumstances that allowed the lost Shan'iatu to bestow perfect and absolute immortality upon their servants cannot be replicated. There will never be new Arisen.<br /> The Bodhisattvas choose not accept this as fact. The Shan'iatu might be dead, but there are other wielders of magic. Lesser forms of immortality exist - vampirism, lichdom, the Sadikh state of being, etc.. The Bodhisattvas believe that, with the correct piece of occult information and the use of the right relics, they will find a way to recreate the occult works of the Shan'iatu, giving true immortality to others.<br /> Their reasons are many - some wish to share their existence with others in ways that even the Sadikh rite and its forced obedience cannot do justice. Some seek a way to pass on their Curse to others, freeing them from their existence as servants for all eternity by swapping places with other, new slaves. All devote much of their time to investigating the secrets of the Rite of Return, despite the obvious disapproval of the Judges of Duat.<br /> <br /> PSYCHOPOMPS<br /> More than anyone else, the Arisen understand the transience of existence. It doesn't do good to be attached to things of this world. All things eventually pass away; attachment only causes pain at some point.<br /> The Psychopomps, composed mainly of Su Menent priests of death, see it as their moral obligation to teach timeless truths about transience and death to the living. Sometimes they do this by setting drastic examples, killing people and destroying homes in an effort to make philosophical points; sometimes they comfort broken souls and hearts. All of them espouse philosophies of letting go of material possessions, finding truth in matters of the spirit.<br /> <br /> THE HOPEFUL<br /> Jill works in a hardware store, selling locks 9-5. Not all her customers are alive. She definitely prefers the living - but the shop still adequately fulfills many needs.<br /> After work hours, there's a date with Bill, the guy who kissed her to life and brought her back. It's so romantic, her friends always tell her. Like Sleeping Beauty. Jill has no idea what that is.<br /> Jill sometimes dreams of a past that she has buried so deep that she often has trouble accessing it - of a war between the Hycsos and the Egyptians; of the Christians being persecuted in Rome; of the Irish being slaughtered in Dublin not so long ago. She does her best to ignore all of that. This is not her life any more.<br /> She won't be alive for long. All Arisen live on borrowed time. Some time soon, she'll fall ill and die. There will be another someone like Bill when she comes back, and she'll fall in love again. Isn't that romantic?<br /> Some Arisen just want to be left alone and live as mortals. The desire to ignore their duties to the Judges and just *live* is a strong one. It never really works out. There's Sybaris. The opposition of pretty much every mummy still loyal to the Judges, in addition to everything else that hates and fears the Arisen.<br /> And, obviously, the vast cultural difficulties of a being from the stone age trying to live in the 21st century.<br /> <br /> PAKHET<br /> Some people don't deserve to live. Individual destinies may, in the long run, prove harmful to Fate and to the human race, if left unchecked.<br /> The Pakhet are a cult of mummies who bring the gift of death to those deserving. As beings intimately familiar with death, possessing the wisdom of 6000 years, they see themselves as capable of making the decision of who deserves to live and who must die.<br /> All mummies die at the end of their Descent. The Pakhet see this as a sort of penance; often allowing mortal hunters of supernatural nightmares 